{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01091 - MONDO_1461",
  "verbalization": "Butenafine, a topical antifungal agent, is implicated in the treatment of Tinea Corporis through a mechanism of action that involves the inhibition of fungal growth. The evidence suggests that Butenafine may be repurposed for Tinea Corporis, as it is indicated for Pityriasis Versicolor, a related fungal infection, and these conditions share a common pathway of fungal infection. The indication of Butenafine for Pityriasis Versicolor, which is treated with oxiconazole, sulconazole, and econazole, all of which are antifungal agents, supports the hypothesis that Butenafine may exert a similar antifungal effect on Tinea Corporis. The high scores in the visible paths indicate a strong, consistent relationship between Butenafine and Tinea Corporis, suggesting a plausible mechanistic link. The integration of these paths highlights the potential for Butenafine to be used in the treatment of Tinea Corporis through antifungal activity, supported by the shared indication and pathway of fungal infections.",
